{% if include_lore %}### 2026-11-07 — [variable] CLI `--help` on a subcommand can execute the command

- Question: which `--help` invocations are safe to use as interface probes?
- Setup: each command run once against a pinned release, output and exit status
  recorded. The same measurement was taken independently in a chezmoi-managed
  topic and in a stamped project; both agreed.
- Result: `--help` is handled only at the top level. With a subcommand present
  the flag falls through to the normal argument parser, and behaviour depends on
  the command. Measured on a CLI at version 0.40.0: its `setup --help` ran
  setup; its `data export --help` ran the export; its `doctor --help` ran
  read-only diagnostics but exited 1 on a failing check; its `data list --help`
  and `recall --help` failed with usage errors. Two mutate state and report
  success — indistinguishable from an intentional run.
- Verdict: promote-to-tooling. Encoded as a rule in `.context/ai-rules.md`, and
  version-pinned here because the underlying defect belongs to the tool and may
  be fixed in a later release. Re-measure on upgrade.

The same tool properties that make a `--help` probe unsafe also make its
knowledge file unsafe to hand-edit: it is a render of a database, and the next
export discards anything the database never held. Record that as a `[variable]`
entry when you first hit it, so the next person inherits the measurement rather
than repeating it.
